(* newspeak-spec.pdf 6.2: "Class I has a primary factory method (6) with the
   same selector as m", and "The instance initializer of M is then evaluated
   on o with the arguments of mu_m." Marker's primary factory is withTag:,
   so a <: clause naming Marker sends withTag: and its argument reaches
   Marker's initializer as aTag.

   The applying class's own factory is its own message pattern, not the
   clause's: Applied declares none, so it is new and Applied is instantiated
   with Applied new, while its level still receives withTag: 'x'. Tagged
   declares label:, and its clause passes that factory parameter on (6.3:
   "the bindings of the formal parameters of the factory method are
   available in the message clause"), so Tagged label: 'y' has tag 'y'. Each
   instance prints its own tag; an implementation that sent the level new
   instead would fail 6.1's primary factory check, or leave the tag nil. *)
class MixinAppliedLevelTakesItsClausesFactorySelector = Object new () (
    class Marker withTag: aTag = Object new (| public tag = aTag. |) (
        public describe = ( ^'tagged ', tag )
    ) : ()
    class Applied = Object new <: Marker withTag: 'x'.
    class Tagged label: aLabel = Object new <: Marker withTag: aLabel (
    ) (
        public describeLabel = ( ^'Tagged, ', describe )
    ) : ()
    public main: platform args: arguments = (
        | t = platform transcript. |
        t show: 'Applied new is ', Applied new describe; newLine.
        t show: 'Applied new tag is ', Applied new tag; newLine.
        t show: (Tagged label: 'y') describeLabel; newLine.
        t show: 'Tagged label: ''z'' tag is ', (Tagged label: 'z') tag; newLine.
    )
) : ()
